{"qid":"Q1232887","names":["County of Savoy"],"title":"County of Savoy","endonym":null,"capsule":"The County of Savoy was a feudal state within the Holy Roman Empire created when Humbert I 'White Hands' received the comital title from Burgundy's last king Rudolph III around 1003, controlling Alpine passes linking Italy and France. Amadeus V 'the Great' (r. 1285–1323) expanded the county considerably, while Amadeus VIII acquired the County of Nice in 1388 and purchased the County of Geneva in 1401; Chambéry served as capital from 1295. The county gained imperial immediacy in 1313 and was elevated by Holy Roman Emperor Sigismund to a duchy in 1416, with Amadeus VIII becoming the first Duke of Savoy.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The county's core Alpine territory is well established in medieval charters; precise outer boundaries shifted repeatedly through feudal grants and acquisitions and are mapped only approximately.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: County of Savoy","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_Savoy"},{"label":"Britannica: House of Savoy","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/House-of-Savoy"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"County of Savoy","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Boniface of Savoy","nativeName":"Boniface de Savoie","roman":"Boniface","start":1253,"end":1263,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Peter II of Savoy","nativeName":"Pierre II de Savoie","roman":"Peter II","start":1263,"end":1268,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Philip I of Savoy","nativeName":"Philippe Ier de Savoi